{
  "sn12.25:0.1": "Linked Discourses 12.25 ",
  "sn12.25:0.2": "3. The Ten Powers ",
  "sn12.25:0.3": "With Bhūmija ",
  "sn12.25:1.1": "At Sāvatthī. ",
  "sn12.25:1.2": "Then in the late afternoon, Venerable Bhūmija came out of retreat, went to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. ",
  "sn12.25:1.3": "When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to him: ",
  "sn12.25:2.1": "“Reverend Sāriputta, there are ascetics and brahmins who teach the efficacy of deeds. Some of them assert that pleasure and pain are made by oneself. ",
  "sn12.25:2.2": "Some of them assert that pleasure and pain are made by another. ",
  "sn12.25:2.3": "Some of them assert that pleasure and pain are made by both oneself and another. ",
  "sn12.25:2.4": "Some of them assert that pleasure and pain arise anomalously, not made by oneself or another. ",
  "sn12.25:2.5": "What does the Buddha say about this? How does he explain it? ",
  "sn12.25:2.6": "How should we answer so as to repeat what the Buddha has said, and not misrepresent him with an untruth? How should we explain in line with his teaching, with no legitimate grounds for rebuttal and criticism?” ",
  "sn12.25:3.1": "“Reverend, the Buddha has said that pleasure and pain are dependently originated. ",
  "sn12.25:3.2": "Dependent on what? ",
  "sn12.25:3.3": "Dependent on contact. ",
  "sn12.25:3.4": "In speaking like this, you would repeat what the Buddha has said, and not misrepresent him with an untruth. You w